# Sitrifor Web UI Rule

Before changing UI in `public/**/*.{html,css,js}`:
- identify the user scenario and the main CTA;
- identify the visual source of truth: screenshots, design system, existing code, or agreed reference;
- check mobile order, empty/loading/success/error states, and dead-end flows;
- decide whether the task needs no motion, subtle polish, or a dedicated motion concept;
- confirm button CSS is loaded on the target page (guides must not rely on `yandex-business.css` unless linked).

When implementing:
- prefer clear visual hierarchy over decorative complexity;
- do not mix unrelated jobs-to-be-done in one screen or tab set;
- keep one dominant action per viewport section;
- preserve responsive readability, spacing rhythm, and tap target quality;
- for images: match CSS max size to aspect ratio (phone ≠ square marketing);
- remove orphan HTML/CSS/JS references when deleting UI;
- for Sitrifor's static frontend, default to lightweight CSS/JS motion, not heavy animation tooling.

After implementing:
- run a UX pass for hierarchy, affordance, copy clarity, dead CTAs, and scroll behavior;
- verify computed contrast on CTAs and that media does not eat a full viewport scroll;
- verify on the actual page, not just in code;
- if motion was added, check `prefers-reduced-motion`.
